So what this will be is 0.

00:16:19.559 --> 00:16:22.759
1228 and essentially
that'd be divided by 62.

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:24.829
03 divided by 1000.

00:16:25.609 --> 00:16:34.520
So when we solve for that, what
we will get Is 1.98 mole, and

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:41.349
we'll put that in that cursive M
and that refers to 1.98 moles of

00:16:41.349 --> 00:16:45.339
sucrose over one kilogram of water.

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:46.400
Okay?

00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:51.860
So, and that is how we would solve
this problem here is just like that.

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:56.480
Let's go on to another
example using modality.

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:59.930
If the modality of KCL is five mole.

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:04.940
How many ions does KCl break
into when it's dissolved?

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:09.200
And then I asked, what is the
total molality of the solution?

00:17:09.639 --> 00:17:10.579
So this is a two parter.

00:17:11.339 --> 00:17:13.460
So KCl, how does that dissolve?

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:18.459
So it goes off your solubility rules
in understanding how all that works.

00:17:18.929 --> 00:17:25.310
So if I have KCl, which is aqueous,
that's going to break down into our

00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:29.149
cation and our anion, which are K plus.

00:17:29.794 --> 00:17:32.135
and Cl minus, right?

00:17:33.024 --> 00:17:34.945
So how many ions do you have?

00:17:35.014 --> 00:17:39.484
Well, looking at that, you
have one, two ions total.

00:17:40.054 --> 00:17:41.955
So what does that refer to?

00:17:41.965 --> 00:17:44.895
Well, that refers to the second
part of the problem, which

00:17:44.935 --> 00:17:47.155
asks for the total molality.

00:17:47.764 --> 00:17:51.965
Now, one thing about molality, it
refers to how many ions are in solution.

00:17:51.975 --> 00:17:56.215
It's about colligative properties,
which is all about how ions

00:17:56.245 --> 00:17:58.580
affect different properties.

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:03.179
So one thing that gets affected
is depending on how many ions

00:18:03.189 --> 00:18:07.499
there are, that concentrate, that
concentration above can change.

00:18:08.159 --> 00:18:12.979
So there's five molal of KCl,
but the total is essentially

00:18:13.009 --> 00:18:14.599
times the number of ions.

00:18:15.279 --> 00:18:22.255
So if I have two ions in solution, What
I'm going to simply do is times that

00:18:22.255 --> 00:18:31.274
by 5 molal, and that's going to equal a
total of 10 molal, or the total molality

00:18:31.334 --> 00:18:37.200
for all ions in the solution, and that's
because it dissociated it into two pieces.

00:18:37.639 --> 00:18:41.210
So if you had something that's
associated into three pieces,

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:44.860
you would times it by three, four
pieces of times by four and so on.

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:49.820
So it's all about how many ions
that are in, in the actual solution.

00:18:50.179 --> 00:18:54.019
So it's a pretty easy concept,
but it's kind of strange just

00:18:54.029 --> 00:18:57.080
because it's, it's different than
any of the other concentrations.

00:18:57.749 --> 00:19:00.529
Um, so it's all about
the ions specifically.

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:04.080
Let's look at our last
type of concentration.

00:19:04.179 --> 00:19:06.980
Our last type of
concentration is molarity.

00:19:07.409 --> 00:19:12.980
Which, what molarity is, so molarity, so
with the R focused this time, so the L,

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:19.139
it's the amount of moles of the solute
over the volume of solution, so it's

00:19:19.179 --> 00:19:21.540
all about the liquid side of it instead.

00:19:22.165 --> 00:19:24.375
So, now why is this one so important?

00:19:24.445 --> 00:19:28.635
It's probably, it's arguably
the most common concentration

00:19:28.635 --> 00:19:30.945
that's used in a chemistry lab.

00:19:31.364 --> 00:19:36.714
So if you have to use this in, for
future chemistry classes, so with general

00:19:36.715 --> 00:19:42.905
chemistry, analytical chemistry, physical,
organic, you will be using this a lot.

00:19:43.185 --> 00:19:46.035
So I recommend mastering it and
getting comfortable with it.

